.\" hydra-host(1)
.TH HYDRA\-HOST 1 "2026-08-30" "hydra" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
hydra-host \- native-messaging bridge between the browser extension and the Hydra app
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hydra\-host
.PP
Not run by hand.
The browser spawns it, speaks native messaging on its standard input and
output, and reads nothing else.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Hydra browser extension captures downloads, offers
.RB \(lq "Download with Hydra" \(rq
menu entries, and
.I sniffs media
on each tab: direct audio and video responses, and HLS
.RB ( .m3u8 )
and DASH
.RB ( .mpd )
manifests, which it lists with their renditions so one can be picked and
handed to the app.
.B hydra\-host
is one of the two ways that reaches the running application.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
                +-- WebSocket 127.0.0.1:6799 -----------+   primary
extension ------+                                       +--> hydra\-gui
                +-- native messaging --> hydra\-host ----+   fallback
                    (stdio frames)      starts the app
.fi
.RE
.PP
The WebSocket is the primary transport: no process spawn per request, fixed
ports
.RB ( 6799 ,
falling back to
.BR 16799 ),
authenticated by the extension origin, and the live socket is itself the
\(lqHydra is running\(rq indicator the toolbar icon shows.
.PP
.B hydra\-host
is the fallback, and the only path that can
.I start
the app.
It reads one native-messaging frame at a time \(em a 4-byte little-endian
length followed by a JSON object \(em forwards it to the running
.BR hydra\-gui
over the loopback line protocol, and frames the reply back the same way.
The port and a random token come from
.IR ipc.json ,
which the app publishes in its config directory; the token is added by this
process, so the browser never sees it and a page cannot forge a request to
the app.
.PP
When no app is running, every request except
.B ping
launches
.B hydra\-gui
minimized and waits up to 20 seconds for the socket to appear \(em clicking a
download in the browser starts Hydra and then completes, rather than failing
because the app was closed.
.B ping
deliberately never launches anything: it exists to
.I probe
whether the app is up.
A request that finds no app at all is answered
.BR "{\(dqok\(dq:false,\(dqerror\(dq:\(dqhydra is not running\(dq}" ,
and malformed JSON with
.BR \(dqbad\ json\(dq .
One connection is kept across frames, and a stale one (the app was
restarted) is dropped and retried once.
.
.SH REGISTRATION
A browser only speaks to this binary if it finds a manifest naming it, under
the host name
.BR com.hydra.host .
.PP
.B The application writes those manifests itself, at every start,
for every browser already present on the machine \(em all the locations are
per-user, nothing needs administrator rights, and an unchanged manifest is
left alone.
Normally there is nothing to do.
From a source checkout, where the app may never have been launched, the same
job is done by
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
scripts/install\-native\-host.sh                       (macOS, Linux)
powershell \-ExecutionPolicy Bypass \e
    \-File scripts\(rsinstall\-native\-host.ps1          (Windows)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Chromium-family browsers allow-list an extension
.IR origin ,
Firefox an add-on
.IR id ;
both installers derive them from the extension manifests
.RI ( key ,
pinned so the id is stable, and
.BR browser_specific_settings.gecko.id ),
so the two sides cannot drift apart.
Restart the browser once after a manifest is written.
.PP
A browser that has never been launched has no profile directory, and so gets
nothing written into it; start it once and register again.
Safari has no native-messaging registry at all \(em its extension talks to
the containing app through
.BR SFSafariWebExtensionHandler ,
and Apple exposes no download events to extensions there, so automatic
capture is a Chromium and Firefox feature.
.
.SH PROTOCOL
Requests are JSON objects with a
.B type
and an optional
.BR id ,
echoed back so replies can be matched:
.TP
.B ping
Is the app up?
Answered without launching it.
.TP
.B config
Return the current capture settings and nothing else.
.TP
.B open
Bring the app's window to the front.
.TP
.B download
.RI { url ", " filename ", " cookies ", " referer ", " user_agent ", " size ,
.IR mime ", " tab_url }
\(em a captured file.
.TP
.B links
.RI { urls }
\(em several URLs at once, from
.RB \(lq "Download all links" \(rq
or a selection.
.TP
.B stream
.RI { url ", " protocol ", " variant_url ", " variant ", " container ,
.IR filename ", " cookies ", " referer ", " user_agent ", " tab_url ", " live ,
.IR duration }
\(em a sniffed HLS or DASH manifest with the rendition and container the user
chose.
A manifest is not a file, so this is a distinct request type: handing it to
the download path would save a few kilobytes of playlist text.
.PP
Every reply carries
.BR ok ,
the current
.BR capture ,
.BR auto_types " and " dont_start_sites
settings, the app
.BR version ,
the request
.BR id ,
and
.B error
when something failed.
The settings ride along on every reply so the file-type list, the excluded
sites, and this browser's own capture checkbox propagate from the app's
Options window to the extension without a separate channel.
.PP
Requests naming a rendition are refused before they are sent when the
manifest declares Widevine, PlayReady, FairPlay or MPEG common encryption:
such a stream is listed as protected and is never handed to the app.
See
.BR STREAMS " in " hydra (1)
for what the engine then does with the ones that are sent.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HYDRA_GUI_BIN
Path to the
.B hydra\-gui
binary to launch, tried before anything else.
Without it, launching tries the installed
.I "Hydra Download Manager"
application bundle (macOS), then a
.B hydra\-gui
beside this binary, then
.B hydra\-gui
on
.BR PATH .
On macOS the bundle is deliberately first: it carries the identity the user
granted folder access to, where a bare sibling binary is refused
.IR ~/Downloads .
.TP
.BR HOME ", " APPDATA
Locate the config directory holding
.IR ipc.json :
.I $HOME/.config/hydra
everywhere but Windows, where it is
.IR %APPDATA%\(rshydra .
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hydra/ipc.json
Published by the running app: the loopback port and the shared token this
host authenticates with.
Never read by the browser.
On Windows,
.IR %APPDATA%\(rshydra\(rsipc.json .
.TP
.IR ".../NativeMessagingHosts/com.hydra.host.json"
The manifest naming this binary, one per browser profile root.
Chromium keeps it inside the profile directory, Firefox in Mozilla's own
.I native\-messaging\-hosts
directory, and Windows browsers replace the file with an
.B HKCU
key pointing at one under
.IR %LOCALAPPDATA%\(rsHydra .
.
.SH NOTES
Frames larger than 64 MB, or of zero length, are treated as framing
corruption: the process exits and lets the browser respawn it.
.PP
Only one job actually needs this binary \(em starting Hydra when it is not
already running.
Everything else goes over the WebSocket, which behaves identically on macOS,
Linux and Windows and needs no registration.
Since the app installs a login item by default, a normally-configured
machine reaches the host only on a cold start.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hydra (1),
.BR hydra\-interactive (1)
.PP
